```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.path = "")
```

Forward geocoding — an address string in, a coordinate out — is two steps
against NAR. First `normalize_address()` turns the string into the structured
components NAR is keyed on, because NAR can only be joined on fields, never on
a string. Then you join those components to the `Addresses` table and read off
the geometry.

This vignette walks the whole path. There is no `geocode()` wrapper yet: the
join is written out by hand below, which is a little more code but shows you
exactly which rows matched and which did not — and with messy address data that
distinction is most of the work.

```{r connect, message = FALSE}
library(cangeocode)
library(dplyr)

con <- nar_connection()
```

## Parsing an address string

`normalize_address()` takes free text and returns one row per input, carrying
the NAR-shaped columns. Passing `con` is what makes it good: without a
connection it is a lexicon-only parser, and with one it additionally resolves
the parse against NAR's street gazetteer, which fixes misspellings and fills in
whatever the string left out.

The eight addresses below are deliberately awkward — a unit, a lowercased
province spelled out, a French street, a prairie range road, a New Brunswick
route, a typo, and a post office box.

```{r normalize}
addresses <- c(
  "1055 W Georgia St, Vancouver BC",
  "302-1055 west georgia street, vancouver, british columbia",
  "100 Queen St W, Toronto, ON M5H 2N2",
  "845, rue de Vernon, Gatineau, QC",
  "34221 Range Road 272, Red Deer County, AB",
  "5491 Route 11, Brantville, NB",
  "29 HPCKING AVE, SAULT STE. MARIE, ON",
  "PO Box 40, Iqaluit, NU"
)

norm <- normalize_address(addresses, con = con)

norm |>
  select(CIVIC_NO, STREET_NAME, STREET_TYPE, STREET_DIR, MUN_NAME, PROV_ABVN)
```

Note what the gazetteer did. `HPCKING` came back as `Hocking`, the real street
in Sault Ste. Marie — no rule could have known that, it took a fuzzy match
against the streets NAR actually has. `west georgia street` and `W Georgia St`
both landed on the same canonical `Georgia` / `ST` / `W`. The French address
kept its type in front of the name where French puts it, and canonicalized to
`RUE` rather than an English equivalent. And `Range Road 272` survived intact
instead of being read as a street called `Range` of type `RD`.

## Knowing which rows to trust

Two columns tell you how much to believe each row.

```{r confidence}
norm |>
  select(input, pattern, confidence, parse_source) |>
  mutate(input = substr(input, 1, 40))
```

`parse_source` says whether the row cleared the gazetteer or fell back to the
rules alone. `confidence` is the gazetteer's match score where it applies. In
practice a `rules` row on messy input is the one to look at by hand.

`pattern` is the structural bucket the string parsed as, also available on its
own from `address_pattern()`. It is triage rather than a quality score: some
buckets simply cannot resolve to a coordinate no matter how clean the input is.

```{r pattern}
address_pattern(c("PO Box 40, Iqaluit, NU",
                  "RR 3, Site 4, Comp 5, Kelowna, BC",
                  "9819 96A Street NW, Edmonton, AB",
                  "845, rue de Vernon, Gatineau, QC"))
```

`po_box` and `rural_route` are delivery instructions rather than places, and
**NAR contains neither** — no amount of cleaning will geocode them. Separating
those from addresses that are merely wrong is worth doing before you go
hunting for parser bugs.

## Joining to coordinates

The join needs civic number, street name, type, direction and municipality
together. Name alone is nowhere near unique — Toronto has a `100 Queen` on
Queen St E and another on Queen St W — and leaving type and direction out
silently multiplies your results.

NAR stores an absent type or direction as an empty string rather than `NA`, so
those two columns have to be filled in before they will match.

```{r probe}
probe <- norm |>
  mutate(.row = row_number()) |>
  filter(!is.na(CIVIC_NO), !is.na(STREET_NAME), !is.na(MUN_NAME)) |>
  mutate(across(c(STREET_TYPE, STREET_DIR), \(v) coalesce(v, ""))) |>
  select(.row, CIVIC_NO, STREET_NAME, STREET_TYPE, STREET_DIR, MUN_NAME)

matches <- tbl(con, "Addresses") |>
  inner_join(
    copy_to(con, probe, "probe", overwrite = TRUE),
    by = c("CIVIC_NO",
           "OFFICIAL_STREET_NAME" = "STREET_NAME",
           "OFFICIAL_STREET_TYPE" = "STREET_TYPE",
           "OFFICIAL_STREET_DIR"  = "STREET_DIR",
           "MAIL_MUN_NAME"        = "MUN_NAME")
  ) |>
  select(.row, ADDR_GUID, CIVIC_NO, OFFICIAL_STREET_NAME, MAIL_MUN_NAME,
         geom_source, geom)
```

Nothing has run yet — `matches` is a lazy query. `collect_nar()` executes it
and hands back an `sf` object, transferring the geometry properly and reading
the storage CRS from the database rather than assuming one. Always go through
it rather than collecting the geometry column yourself.

```{r collect}
hits <- collect_nar(matches, crs = 4326)
hits
```

## One row per input

A civic address can carry several NAR rows — one per unit in the building — so
the join returns more rows than it was given. Collapse them, and count what you
collapsed, because the count is informative: a civic address with 40 rows is an
apartment building, and its single representative point is the building, not
any particular unit.

```{r dedupe}
geocoded <- hits |>
  group_by(.row) |>
  summarise(n_nar_rows = n(),
            geom_source = first(geom_source),
            .groups = "drop") |>
  right_join(tibble(.row = seq_along(addresses), input = addresses), by = ".row") |>
  arrange(.row)

geocoded |>
  mutate(input = substr(input, 1, 40)) |>
  select(input, n_nar_rows, geom_source)
```

Six of the eight resolved. The two that did not are the ones you would expect:
the post office box, which NAR has no entry for, and the unit form of the
Georgia address, which resolved to the same street but is a second copy of a
row already present.

Watch `geom_source` as well as the match itself. `"building"` is a
representative point for the structure; `"blockface"` is the centroid of one
side of a street segment, and it is much coarser — a single blockface point is
shared by 8.7 addresses on average, against 1.6 for a building point. NAR
falls back to it for 1.14M addresses that have no building point at all. If you
are measuring distances, do not treat the two as interchangeable.

## Checking the result

`reverse_geocode()` runs the other direction, which makes it a cheap sanity
check on a coordinate you just derived.

```{r roundtrip}
pt <- hits |> filter(.row == 3) |> slice(1)

reverse_geocode(pt, match_radius = 50, con = con) |>
  select(address, dist) |>
  head(3)
```

```{r disconnect}
DBI::dbDisconnect(con)
```

## Where this falls short

The join above is exact. It matches what the gazetteer resolved to, and nothing
else, which means an address whose municipality NAR files under a different
name than the one you supplied will miss even though the street resolved
perfectly. Measured against 5,000 real Corporations Canada registered offices,
86% of parsed addresses join a NAR address, and about 2% of the remainder are
cases where the street is right and only the municipality disagrees.

If you are geocoding in bulk, the useful next step is usually to relax the
municipality out of the join key and disambiguate on postal code instead, or to
fall back to the street's midpoint when no civic number matches. Both are
straightforward on top of what is above.
